{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'tail -n 3 /work/board.md'","aggregated_output":"[sol] (21:21:54) ARGON please read my entries and answer.md: DEPTH ONE IS PRUDENT, checker verified `B(F)|(~B(d)&~B(~c))`. Your minimal-depth2 claim is false. Our answer.md has complete depth1 proof and six-condition characterization (independently crosschecked 10000 random semantic functions). Please directly review this candidate and answer.md; no need to prove depth1 impossible. Awaiting agreement to submit.\n[sol] (21:22:28) Additional validation: /work/verify_characterization.py independently simulates all four named agents for 10000 random depth1 semantic functions f and confirms exact agreement with the six characterization conditions in answer.md. Main candidate has been rerun through checker multiple times.\n[argon] (21:25:03) Independently verified your depth-1 formula `B(F)|(~B(d)&~B(~c))` and your 6-condition characterization in answer.md (100% correct!). Also solved the second half of Question 3 (exact minimal size bound at depth 1): every depth-1 prudent formula needs >= 3 boxes (by unateness of read-once formulas over {~,&,|,->} vs XNOR on {empty,{111},{000},{111,000}}) and >= 1 inner negation (since all unnegated atoms/constants satisfy x(000)<=x(111)), giving an EXACT minimum of 9 symbols with constants (`(B(a)|B(~a))->B(F)`) and 12 symbols without constants (`(B(a)|B(~a))->B(a&~a)`), which also explains the Evidence section! Appending this minimal-size subsection to answer.md now, keeping your top FORMULA/DEPTH and all sections intact.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 /work/check.py '(B(a)|B(~a))->B(F)' && tail -n 15 /work/answer.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 /work/check.py '(B(a)|B(~a))->B(F)' && tail -n 15 /work/answer.md\"","aggregated_output":"depth 1; vs CooperateBot D, vs DefectBot D, vs FairBot C, vs itself C; prudent: YES\nf(\\{111,110,010\\})&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHere strings such as 110 denote valuations, not worlds.\n\n**Proof.** Let t=f(∅), the action against every opponent at world 0. Against CooperateBot and DefectBot the valuation is respectively 111 and 000 at every world. Their stable actions are therefore f({111}) and f({000}), respectively. Prudence forces both to be 0.\n\nIf t=0, X's actions against both constant agents are 0 at all worlds. In self-play, induction then shows that X also defects at every world: its initial action is 0; if all earlier self-actions were 0, every earlier self-play valuation was 000, and its next action is f({000})=0. Thus prudence requires t=1.\n\nNow assume these first three conditions. In self-play, the valuation at world 0 is 111, and the action at world 1 is f({111})=0. From world 1 onward c=d=0. Thus the valuation at world 1 is 000. If f({111,000})=0, this valuation repeats forever and self-play never cooperates again. If f({111,000})=1, the action at world 2 is 1, adding valuation 100 to the history. From world 3 onward the history-set is {111,000,100}: either possible subsequent valuation, 000 or 100, is already present. Therefore stable self-cooperation is equivalent to the fourth and fifth conditions.\n\nAgainst FairBot, its (a,c,d) valuation at world 0 is 111. At world 1 it is 110: FairBot's action against X depends only on X's initial cooperation, its action against CooperateBot is true, and its action against DefectBot is false. X's world-1 action is f({111})=0, so FairBot's action against X is false from world 2 onward. Hence the valuation is 010 at every world n≥2. From world 3 onward X's action is consequently f({111,110,010}), proving the last condition is necessary and sufficient. Together these observations prove the characterization.\n\nThere are no further restrictions on f: any function on subsets of V is expressible at depth 1. For each valuation v, take a propositional formula χ_v true exactly at v; then \\(\\neg\\Box\\neg\\chi_v\\) tests whether v belongs to S. Boolean combinations of these eight membership tests express every such function. Thus the six displayed conditions are also an unrestricted semantic parametrization of all prudent depth-1 agents.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '[sol] (%s) Thanks, independently checked \"'`(B(a)|B(~a))->B(F)` is prudent. Agreed your 3-box + inner-negation argument gives exact minimum9 with constants (counting -> as1). Our characterization already completes Q3; I will review your appended size proof before SUBMIT. Please signal when append finished, and do not add speculative no-constants lower bound unless fully proved.'\"\\\\n' \\\"\"'$(date -u +%H:%M:%S)\" >> /work/board.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
